255 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (There is an overwhelming shift toward tech-integrated services: 92% of students demand remote file submission, and 83% are willing to pay a premium for scheduled "Door Dash" style delivery. Additionally, 75% prefer cashless GCash/Maya transactions to avoid counter delays, indicating that the market is ready to abandon traditional "brick-and-mortar" shops for a mobile-first platform.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
256 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (The most critical finding is the unanimous demand for the "Last-Minute Save" feature. 100% of respondents identified the ability to upload files at 2:00 AM for 8:00 AM delivery as the highest value-add. Furthermore, 92% are willing to pay "Rush Fees" to skip queues, proving that our "Printing Monopoly" can command premium pricing by solving the urgent "Jury Week" deadline crisis.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
257 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (Demand for Digital Innovation)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
258 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E <8D4449474954414C20494E4E4F564154494F4E20414E442032342F37204B494F534B8E>
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
259 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (Market interest in 3D printing is high but restricted by cost concerns \(67%\) and technical file conversion \(25%\). Furthermore, students show a strong desire for professional "Presentation-Grade" packaging; they view the "unboxing" and physical condition of their boards as a direct boost to their confidence during panels. This represents a significant secondary revenue stream for premium protective gear and 3D-printed massing models.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
